# Environments — author here, build and flash there

This project spans **two machines**. Getting this wrong wastes a session, so assume
nothing and check.

| | Workstation (this workspace) | Heliotrope Ridge (build host) |
|---|---|---|
| Role | Author code, docs, specs, git, `gh` | Compile, flash, serial capture, soak |
| Path | `~/Documents/Brandt/rak-sensor-node-but-better` | `~/Documents/GitHub/lorawan/rak-sensor-node-but-better` |
| Reach | local | `ssh "$RAK_BUILD_HOST"` — **you must set this, see below** |
| PlatformIO | **absent** | `/opt/homebrew/bin/pio` |
| `gh` CLI | present | `/opt/homebrew/bin/gh`, `GITHUB_TOKEN` in `~/.zprofile` |
| USB / DFU | **no device** — cannot flash | **the only machine with the RAK4631 on USB** |
| Writes outside the repo | **blocked** (agent sandbox: `$HOME` is read-only) | unrestricted |

## The rule

**Author locally. Compile and flash on Heliotrope Ridge. Always.**

Do not try to install PlatformIO, toolchains, or nRF utilities on the workstation. The
agent sandbox denies writes to `$HOME`, so `~/.platformio` cannot be created — and even
if it could, the hardware is not on this machine's USB. Anything that touches the
physical device is a build-host operation, full stop.

If a tool is genuinely missing on **both** machines, install it on the **build host** and
record it in `docs/ENVIRONMENTS.md`. Never install project toolchains on the workstation.

## The build host is a laptop — set the address, do not investigate it

**The build host is the operator's laptop, and it is not always on the same network.** That
is the whole story. An SSH failure is a fact about where the laptop is, not evidence of a
broken host, a dead machine, or an infrastructure problem. **Ask for the address. Do not
open an investigation.**

No address is recorded in this repository, because the repository is public.

```bash
export RAK_BUILD_HOST=ntableman@<address>       # ask the operator for <address>
ssh -o ConnectTimeout=8 -o BatchMode=yes "$RAK_BUILD_HOST" 'zsh -l -c "hostname"'
# → Heliotrope-Ridge
```

Every script resolves the host as `BUILD_HOST`, then `RAK_BUILD_HOST`, then the first line
of `~/.rak-build-host` — an untracked file, which is the way to make it persist across
shells without putting the address in the repo. **There is no default.** With none of the
three set, the scripts fail immediately naming `RAK_BUILD_HOST`, rather than connecting to a
guess and reporting the result as a dead host. Never paste the address into a commit, an
issue, a doc, or a comment; `scripts/preflight.sh` fails on any IPv4 literal in a tracked
file.

## The SSH gotcha that will bite you

**Non-interactive SSH does not source `~/.zprofile`, so `/opt/homebrew/bin` is not on
`PATH`.** A bare `ssh host 'pio run'` reports `pio: command not found` and looks exactly
like "PlatformIO is not installed." It is installed. Your shell is just wrong.

Always force a login shell:

```bash
ssh "$RAK_BUILD_HOST" 'zsh -l -c "cd ~/Documents/GitHub/lorawan/rak-sensor-node-but-better && pio run"'
```

Use `scripts/remote.sh` instead of hand-rolling this — it handles the login shell, the
`cd`, and the sentinel lines. Diagnose a "missing" tool with `zsh -l -c "command -v <tool>"`
before concluding anything.

## Git is the only transport between machines

The user sometimes works directly on the build host, or on a laptop. **Never assume the
build host mirrors this workspace.** Uncommitted local edits do not exist over there.

Before any remote build:

1. Commit and push locally.
2. `git -C <remote path> pull --ff-only` on the build host.
3. Confirm the build host HEAD equals the SHA you intend to build, and **report that SHA**.

`scripts/remote.sh sync` does this and refuses to proceed on a dirty remote tree.

Never `scp`, `rsync`, or paste files between machines — that creates an untracked
divergence nobody can reproduce later. If the build host has local commits, stop and
surface it; do not rebase or discard the user's work on the other machine.

## Two GitHub identities — this is normal, not an anomaly

The user has a work account and a personal account. This is settled; do not re-derive it
every session, and **do not treat commits you did not write as suspicious.** It is the
user's repository and they push to it directly.

| Where | Authenticates as | Can push to `disruptivepatternmaterial`? |
|---|---|---|
| Workstation `gh` + HTTPS credential helper | `disruptivepatternmaterial` | **Yes** — re-verified 2026-08-28 |
| Workstation SSH keys (`ssh -T git@github.com`) | `ntableman_sfemu` (work) | No — HTTP 403 |
| The user's IDE (its own GitHub sign-in, not shared with the git CLI) | personal | Yes |
| Heliotrope Ridge build host | `disruptivepatternmaterial` | Yes |

**This rule used to say the workstation could not push at all. That is stale.** The 403 is
real but belongs to the **SSH** keys; `origin` is an HTTPS remote and uses the credential
helper, which is signed in as `disruptivepatternmaterial`. Verify before relying on it —
`git push --dry-run origin HEAD:refs/heads/main` authenticates fully and transfers nothing —
then push. Do not "fix" anything by editing the remote URL or the git config.

`scripts/push.sh` still works and is worth using when the credential helper is uncooperative
or a soak is running: it pushes the branch to a non-checked-out ref on the build host,
fast-forwards the build host's `main`, and pushes to GitHub over SSH. It is no longer the
only way, so an unreachable build host does not strand a commit.

Two consequences worth internalizing:

- **Unfamiliar commits on `origin/main` are expected.** The user commits from their IDE,
  sometimes mid-session and sometimes capturing an agent's in-progress working tree.
  Fetch, look, and continue. Do not narrate it as a mystery.
- **The user's IDE can commit your unfinished work.** A failing gate can therefore reach
  CI before you have fixed it. Fix forward; do not rewrite the pushed history.

## Reporting

Every build, flash, or soak result must state **which host** it ran on and **which commit
SHA** was built. A result without a host and a SHA is not evidence
(`docs/EVIDENCE.md`).
